{% extends 'admin/_base/commercial_document_view.html.twig' %}
{% block title %}{{ bill.billNumber }} | Admin{% endblock %}

{#
    Joining the shared view frame (matching purchase_order_detail.html.twig's own migration).

    `commercial_document_view.html.twig` is the base Invoice, SalesOrder, Estimate and — since its
    own Phase 1 — PurchaseOrder already share. This is Bill joining it. Nothing about what this
    screen shows or how a bill is priced, taxed, guarded or transitioned changed here; only where
    its markup sits, and two small declutters the move made possible (see below).

    The content-to-block mapping:
      - `hero_eyebrow` / `hero_title` / `hero_status` / `hero_actions` — the eyebrow, bill number,
        status badge and Back button `@Procurement/_nav.html.twig` used to render for this screen.
        `_nav.html.twig` is still used by every OTHER purchase-side screen; this one grew its own
        hero because the frame already draws one.
      - `actionBarTemplate` — points at `@Procurement/_bill_document_action_bar.html.twig`, a small
        Bill-specific partial for the same reason `_document_action_bar.html.twig` exists for PO:
        this document has no real tab bar (there is only one GET route), so the slot's only job is
        the flash messages `_nav.html.twig` also used to render (no app.js hook here, so a flash has
        to be real, visible markup) — PLUS the duplicate-invoice-number and received-elsewhere
        warnings, which are this document's own report-driven notices and belong beside the other
        loud, above-everything-else banners rather than folded into a summary card below them.
      - `summary_info_heading` / `summary_info_rows` — "Received at" and the warehouse/tax-province
        paragraph, with its `#bill-receiving-warehouse` id kept on a wrapper `<div>` (the frame gives
        `summary_info` no id slot of its own), the same shape `#po-delivery-province` uses on the
        purchase order's own detail screen. The paragraph's own "Received at" label is dropped since
        the heading now says it — the one declutter this move makes possible, not a fact removed.
      - `summary_billing` — "Remit to", given its own heading the same way "Order to" is on the
        purchase order's screen, for the same reason: the label was inline prose before, and the
        frame gives every address card a real heading now. Empty entirely when the bill has no
        remit-to address, same as PO's "Order to" panel is never conditional on the vendor having no
        vendor at all.
      - `summary_shipping` — left empty. A bill has no shipping-to-a-customer concept, same ruling
        as the purchase order's screen.
      - `document_actions` — the title-row panel: Balance, the vendor-name-and-total line that used
        to be the hero's own lead sentence (the view frame's hero has no lead slot — nothing else
        here restated the vendor's name, so it is kept as its own sentence rather than dropped), the
        dated/paid/PO-link line, and the Edit / Payments / Print / Download PDF / Raise a debit memo
        / Approve buttons — unchanged.
      - `line_items_heading` / `line_items_table` — "Lines" and its table, unchanged, tfoot and all.
      - `totals` — left EMPTY. This document itemises its money inside the line table's own
        `<tfoot>`, which is exactly the case the frame's own docblock names: "a document that
        itemises its money inside the line table's own tfoot must not be given an empty flex row to
        push it about."
      - `related_documents` — Three-way match, Charges and tax, Notes, Payments, Dispute, Return to
        draft and Void, in their original relative order: this document's own related information
        and transition controls, the same concept the purchase order's screen uses this slot for.
      - `activity_log` — the Timeline table, unchanged. `change_history` is left at the frame's own
        default (the generic AuditLog panel), which this screen did not carry before and now gets
        for free, the same as every other CommercialDocument's detail screen.
#}

{% set document = bill %}
{% set slotPrefix = 'admin_bill_detail' %}
{% set actionBarTemplate = '@Procurement/_bill_document_action_bar.html.twig' %}
{% set actionBarContext = {report: report, bill: bill} %}

{% block hero_eyebrow %}Apps › Inventory › Procurement{% endblock %}
{% block hero_title %}{{ bill.billNumber }}{% endblock %}
{% block hero_status %}{% include 'admin/_partials/document_status.html.twig' with {status: bill.status} only %}{% endblock %}
{% block hero_actions %}<a class="button back-button" href="{{ path('admin_bundle_procurement_index') }}">Back</a>{% endblock %}

{# Where the goods landed, and the province frozen from it (queue item 32). The province is the
   document's OWN snapshot, not a live read of the warehouse: moving a warehouse next year must not
   restate last year's tax, which is the same rule CompanyIdentity states and PurchaseOrder::issue()
   applies to its vendor address. So both are printed — the building it came from, and what this
   bill actually says.

   The warehouse clause below is ASKED, not stated (queue item 65). It used to read "its receiving
   warehouse has no province on file" whenever this bill had no province — so filling the
   warehouse's address in left the screen printing "Received at Main — 1 Walkthrough Way, Surrey,
   BC, V3S 0A1" two lines above a sentence saying that warehouse had no province. The building's
   address is read live, so the sentence about it has to be too, or the page contradicts itself. #}
{% block summary_info_heading %}Received at{% endblock %}
{% block summary_info_rows %}
    {# The remedy inside the shared partial's "no tax province" branch depends on where the gap
       actually is, and stays behind Draft: deriveTaxProvinceFrom() refuses off Draft, so telling
       somebody to re-save a bill that has already been approved would be telling them to do
       something the document forbids.

       A DISPUTED bill carries the same gap and a different first step, appended below rather than
       folded into the shared partial: PurchaseOrder has no Disputed status at all, so this is a
       real VendorBill-only exception, not something `_purchase_tax_province_note_view.html.twig`
       should know how to render. It used to carry no step at all: approve() accepts Disputed, so
       this screen stated a rule, offered the button, and then refused it with a message naming a
       save that save() forbids off Draft. The bill could be neither approved nor fixed.

       This names the extra step rather than the gap — the gap is already stated in the sentence
       directly above, and saying it twice is how one copy goes stale. Which step is ASKED of the
       bill (canReturnToDraft), not derived from a status typed out here, so this sentence and the
       control in the Return to draft panel below cannot offer different answers. #}
    <div id="bill-receiving-warehouse">
        <div>
            {% if bill.warehouse %}
                {{ bill.warehouse.name }}{% if bill.warehouse.addressSummary %} — {{ bill.warehouse.addressSummary }}{% endif %}.
            {% else %}
                no warehouse recorded.
            {% endif %}
            {% include '@Procurement/_purchase_tax_province_note_view.html.twig' with {
                documentNoun: 'bill',
                hasProvince: bill.taxProvince,
                provinceDisplay: bill.taxProvince,
                provinceTail: 'frozen onto this bill when it was entered.',
                warehouse: bill.warehouse,
                showWarehouseClause: bill.warehouse and not bill.warehouse.province,
                warehouseClauseText: 'its receiving warehouse has no province on file',
                cannotFreezeSentence: 'A bill with taxable goods and no province cannot be approved.',
                cannotFreezeHere: true,
                showDraftRemedy: bill.status == 'Draft' and bill.warehouse,
            } only %}
            {% if not bill.taxProvince and bill.status == 'Disputed' %}
                {% if bill.canReturnToDraft %}
                    <strong>Return this bill to draft</strong> — the control is further down this page — then save the draft, which is what takes the province from the warehouse.
                {% else %}
                    {{ bill.currency }} {{ bill.amountPaid|amount }} is paid against this bill, so it cannot be returned to draft. Delete or move that payment on its Payments screen first.
                {% endif %}
            {% endif %}
        </div>
    </div>
{% endblock %}

{# Where paying this bill sends the money, frozen when it was entered (#606). Often a different
   legal entity from the vendor — a factor, a lockbox, a parent's accounts department — which is why
   it is on the bill and not read live off the vendor. #}
{% block summary_billing %}
    {% if bill.remitToAddress %}
        <section class="panel">
            <h2>Remit to</h2>
            <p class="lead">{{ bill.remitToAddress|nl2br }}</p>
        </section>
    {% endif %}
{% endblock %}

{% block document_actions %}
    <section class="panel">
        <div class="panel-title-row">
            <div>
                <h2>Balance {{ bill.currency }} {{ bill.balance|amount }}</h2>
                {# The vendor-and-total line the hero's own lead sentence used to carry — the view
                   frame's hero has no lead slot, and nothing else on this screen restates the
                   vendor's name, so it is kept here rather than dropped. #}
                <p class="lead">{{ bill.vendorName }} &middot; {{ bill.currency }} {{ bill.total|amount }}.</p>
                <p class="lead">
                    Dated {{ bill.documentDate }}{% if bill.dueDate %}, due {{ bill.dueDate }}{% endif %}.
                    {{ bill.currency }} {{ bill.amountPaid|amount }} paid of {{ bill.currency }} {{ bill.total|amount }}, across {{ bill.applications|length }} payment(s).
                    {% if bill.purchaseOrder %}
                        Against <a href="{{ path('admin_bundle_procurement_purchase_order', {id: bill.purchaseOrder.id}) }}">{{ bill.purchaseOrder.poNumber }}</a>.
                    {% else %}
                        <strong>No purchase order to match against.</strong>
                    {% endif %}
                </p>
            </div>
            <div class="panel-actions">
                {% if bill.status == 'Draft' %}
                    <a class="button" href="{{ path('admin_bundle_procurement_bill_edit', {id: bill.id}) }}">Edit</a>
                {% endif %}
                <a class="button" href="{{ path('admin_bundle_procurement_bill_payments', {id: bill.id}) }}">Payments</a>
                {# The same pair, in the same order and with the same words, that every other
                   printable buy-side document carries (_send_purchase_order_actions.html.twig and
                   the RFQ reply's) — the owner's ruling on item 44 is that the control is learned
                   once. There is no Send beside them: a bill is never sent to anybody. #}
                <a class="button" href="{{ path('admin_bundle_procurement_bill_print', {id: bill.id}) }}">Print</a>
                <a class="button" href="{{ path('admin_bundle_procurement_bill_print', {id: bill.id, pdf: 1}) }}">Download PDF</a>
                {# The buy-side mirror of "Raise a credit note" on an invoice. The memo records which
                   bill it came from, which is what lets anybody answer "what is this against?"
                   afterwards — the provenance debit_memo.vendor_bill_id was mapped for. #}
                {% if bill.status != 'Void' %}
                    <a class="button" href="{{ path('admin_bundle_procurement_debit_memo_new', {bill: bill.id}) }}">Raise a debit memo</a>
                {% endif %}
                {% if bill.status in ['Draft', 'Disputed'] %}
                    <form method="post" action="{{ path('admin_bundle_procurement_bill_approve', {id: bill.id}) }}" class="inline-form">
                        {{ csrf_field() }}
                        <button class="button primary" type="submit">Approve for payment</button>
                    </form>
                {% endif %}
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>
{% endblock %}

{# ── Lines ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   What the vendor is charging for, extended, with the tax taken on each row and a foot that
   adds up to the figure stored on the document.

   perLineTax and perLineTaxLabel are positional and come off this bill's own frozen tax_lines
   snapshot, so a rate change tomorrow does not reprice a bill entered today. #}
{% block line_items_heading %}Lines{% endblock %}
{% block line_items_table %}
    {{ include('@Procurement/_purchase_detail_line_table.html.twig', {
        lines: bill.lines,
        perLineTax: perLineTax,
        perLineTaxLabel: perLineTaxLabel,
        emptyMessage: 'This bill has no lines. Edit the draft to enter what the vendor is charging for.',
        tableStyle: 'border-collapse: collapse;',
        showFulfillment: false,
        currency: bill.currency,
        footTemplate: '@Procurement/_bill_lines_foot.html.twig',
        footContext: {bill: bill, chargeRows: chargeRows, taxLines: taxLines},
    }, with_context = false) }}
{% endblock %}

{% block related_documents %}
    {# Named so an assertion — or a link — can reach this section's summary sentence rather than
       the whole page. The sentence below is the one that told a user their draft bill's ten
       exceptions each needed a human, on a document no worklist would ever show them on. #}
    <section class="table-card no-paginate" id="bill-three-way-match">
        <h2>Three-way match</h2>
        <p class="lead">
            Ordered ↔ received ↔ charged, within {{ report.quantityTolerancePercent }}% quantity and
            {{ report.priceTolerancePercent }}% price tolerance.
            {% if report.isAutoApprovable %}
                <strong>Clean.</strong> Approvable without anyone thinking about it.
            {% else %}
                {# The count and the scope it holds for, in one sentence, because they were in two
                   places and disagreed. This match counts the exceptions on THIS bill in whatever
                   state it is in; the Exceptions worklist covers only the bills that count as a
                   charge against the business. "Every one needs a human" is true of a bill that is
                   on that worklist and false of one that is not — nothing is going to pick a draft
                   up off a list it is not on, so the page has to say which of the two it is.

                   `onExceptionWorklist` comes from VendorBillStatus::counts(), the same predicate
                   whose enumeration ExceptionController binds to its query. The status word below is
                   the bill's own, so this renders correctly for Void as well as Draft without either
                   being named here. #}
                <strong id="bill-exception-count">{{ report.exceptionCount }} exception(s).</strong>
                {% if onExceptionWorklist %}
                    Every one needs a human.
                {% else %}
                    <span id="bill-exception-scope">A {{ bill.status }} bill is not on the Exceptions worklist, which covers approved bills only — nobody will pick these up from there.</span>
                {% endif %}
            {% endif %}
        </p>
        <div class="wide-table-wrap"><table class="admin-grid-table">
            <thead><tr><th>Line</th><th>Ordered</th><th>Received</th><th>Billed</th><th>Cost ordered</th><th>Cost billed</th><th>Verdict</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                {% for line in report.lines %}
                    <tr>
                        <td>{{ line.description }}</td>
                        <td>{{ line.quantityOrdered }}</td>
                        <td>{{ line.quantityReceived }}</td>
                        <td>{{ line.quantityBilled }}</td>
                        <td>{{ line.unitCostOrdered ?? '—' }}</td>
                        <td>{{ line.unitCostBilled ?? '—' }}</td>
                        <td>{{ line.describeExceptions }}</td>
                    </tr>
                {% else %}
                    <tr><td colspan="7" class="empty-table-cell">
                        <strong>This bill has no lines.</strong>
                        <span>Edit the draft to enter the charges it covers.</span>
                    </td></tr>
                {% endfor %}
            </tbody>
        </table></div>
    </section>

    <section class="table-card no-paginate">
        <h2>Charges and tax</h2>
        <div class="wide-table-wrap"><table class="admin-grid-table">
            <thead><tr><th>Row</th><th>Kind</th><th>Tax class</th><th>Placement</th><th>Amount</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                {% for charge in chargeRows %}
                    <tr>
                        <td>{{ charge.label }}</td>
                        <td>{{ charge.type }}{{ charge.source == 'manual' ? ' (typed)' : ' (calculated)' }}</td>
                        <td>{{ charge.taxClass }}</td>
                        <td>{{ charge.placement|replace({'_': ' '}) }}</td>
                        <td>{{ bill.currency }} {{ charge.amount|amount }}</td>
                    </tr>
                {% endfor %}
                {% for taxRow in taxLines %}
                    <tr>
                        <td>{{ taxRow.label }}</td>
                        <td>tax{{ taxRow.source == 'manual' ? ' (manual)' : ' (calculated)' }}</td>
                        <td>—</td>
                        <td>tax</td>
                        <td>{{ bill.currency }} {{ taxRow.amount|amount }}</td>
                    </tr>
                {% endfor %}
                {% if chargeRows is empty and taxLines is empty %}
                    <tr><td colspan="5" class="empty-table-cell">
                        <strong>No charges and no tax on this bill.</strong>
                        <span>Edit the draft to add freight, brokerage or the tax the vendor charged.</span>
                    </td></tr>
                {% endif %}
            </tbody>
        </table></div>
        <p class="lead">
            Goods {{ bill.currency }} {{ bill.subtotal|amount }} · tax {{ bill.currency }} {{ bill.tax|amount }} ·
            <strong>total {{ bill.currency }} {{ bill.total|amount }}</strong>.
        </p>
    </section>

    {# ── Notes ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
       Typed on the entry form since #555 into `vendor_bill.notes`, stored, and printed by nothing:
       neither this page nor any other screen in the bundle ever rendered the column, so every note
       anybody has written on a bill went into a hole. Rendered only when there is one — thirteen
       empty rows on a document that has no note reads as data that failed to load. #}
    {% if bill.notes %}
        <section class="panel">
            <h2>Notes</h2>
            <p class="lead">{{ bill.notes|nl2br }}</p>
        </section>
    {% endif %}

    <section class="panel">
        <h2>Payments</h2>
        <p class="lead">
            {{ bill.applications|length }} payment(s) recorded, {{ bill.currency }} {{ bill.amountPaid|amount }} in total, leaving
            a balance of {{ bill.currency }} {{ bill.balance|amount }}. The paid figure is the sum of those rows and nothing
            else holds it, so the two cannot disagree.
        </p>
        <p><a class="button" href="{{ path('admin_bundle_procurement_bill_payments', {id: bill.id}) }}">Record or correct a payment</a></p>
    </section>

    {# Asked of the bill, not compared against a list typed out here. This used to read
       `bill.status in ['Draft', 'Open', 'Partially Paid']` — a third hand-kept copy of the
       guard inside VendorBill::dispute(), alongside the one in ExceptionController. See
       VendorBill::DISPUTABLE_FROM. #}
    {% if bill.canBeDisputed %}
        {# Named so the exceptions screen can land a person on this form rather than at the top of
           the page, and pre-fill it with the finding that sent them here. #}
        <section class="panel" id="dispute">
            <h2>Dispute</h2>
            <p class="lead">Parks the bill out of the payment run. A part payment against a disputed bill does not settle the dispute, so nothing derives it back.</p>
            <form method="post" action="{{ path('admin_bundle_procurement_bill_dispute', {id: bill.id}) }}" class="form-grid">
                {{ csrf_field() }}
                <label class="span-2">What is wrong with it? <input type="text" name="reason" maxlength="255" value="{{ suggestedDisputeReason }}" required></label>
                <div class="form-actions span-2"><button class="button" type="submit">Dispute</button></div>
            </form>
        </section>
    {% endif %}

    {# The third answer to a dispute. Approving it ("we argued, we lost") and voiding it ("the bill
       was wrong") were both on this page already; correcting it was not, and correcting a document
       means editing it, which means Draft. Asked of the bill for the reason the dispute panel above
       is — VendorBill::canReturnToDraft() is returnToDraft()'s own guard, so this control appears
       exactly when pressing it would work. #}
    {% if bill.canReturnToDraft %}
        <section class="panel" id="return-to-draft">
            <h2>Return to draft</h2>
            <p class="lead">Refused once money has gone out against this bill.</p>
            <form method="post" action="{{ path('admin_bundle_procurement_bill_return_to_draft', {id: bill.id}) }}" class="form-grid">
                {{ csrf_field() }}
                <label class="span-2">Reason <input type="text" name="reason" maxlength="255"></label>
                <div class="form-actions span-2"><button class="button" type="submit" id="return-to-draft-submit">Return to draft</button></div>
            </form>
        </section>
    {% endif %}

    {% if bill.status in ['Draft', 'Open', 'Disputed'] and not bill.hasPaidAnything %}
        <section class="panel">
            <h2>Void</h2>
            <p class="lead">For a bill entered in error or a duplicate caught before it was paid. The number survives; nothing is deleted.</p>
            <form method="post" action="{{ path('admin_bundle_procurement_bill_void', {id: bill.id}) }}" class="form-grid">
                {{ csrf_field() }}
                <label class="span-2">Reason <input type="text" name="reason" maxlength="255"></label>
                <div class="form-actions span-2"><button class="button" type="submit">Void</button></div>
            </form>
        </section>
    {% endif %}
{% endblock %}

{# Identified so a test can assert a timeline entry against THIS table rather than against the
   page (#627): every entry's text — a dispute reason, a status change — is also plausible copy
   somewhere else on this screen. #}
{% block activity_log %}
    <section class="table-card no-paginate" id="bill-timeline">
        <h2>Timeline</h2>
        <div class="wide-table-wrap"><table class="admin-grid-table">
            <thead><tr><th>When</th><th>Who</th><th>What</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                {% for log in bill.logs %}
                    <tr><td>{{ log.createdAt|date('Y-m-d H:i') }}</td><td>{{ log.userName ?? 'System' }}</td><td>{{ log.comment }}</td></tr>
                {% else %}
                    <tr><td colspan="3" class="empty-table-cell"><strong>Nothing has happened to this bill yet.</strong></td></tr>
                {% endfor %}
            </tbody>
        </table></div>
        <p class="lead">Written at approval time and never recomputed.</p>
    </section>
{% endblock %}
